@*
    One file's two versions, aligned by heading and read as a document.

    The frame is FileView's, on purpose: the same header over the same scrolling
    body, the same MaxHeight and Fill, the same reserved scrollbar gutter, the
    same focusable body. A pane that shows a file and then a comparison of that
    file should not change its furniture between them, and where a rule is
    identical the selector is added to FileView's own rule in components.css
    rather than copied — FolderView set that precedent, and the reason is that
    three copies drift.

    Presentational. It is handed a ComparedSection and draws it; it does not
    parse, does not read a repository, and does not know where the two versions
    came from. MarkdownCompare.Compare is what produces the tree, and it is a
    pure function a host can call anywhere.

    Everything is rendered through MarkdownView, which emits through the render
    tree's normal escaping and filters link and image URLs by scheme
    (MarkdownRender.IsNavigable). No raw HTML is emitted anywhere here, which
    matters more than usual: the "before" side is by definition text somebody
    has already changed their mind about, and it may have arrived from a
    repository file nobody in this product wrote.

    Colour is a tint and never the carrier. All four semantic tokens are dark
    surfaces — --color-success is #1A3A22 and --color-error is #3D0A0D — so a
    4px gutter stripe in one of them against --color-background #121214 is about
    1.2:1, far under the 3:1 a non-text indicator owes its background. The
    stripe would be invisible and "not by colour alone" would hold only by
    accident. Getting a visible green and red would mean adding palette colours,
    and the library's own token comment records exactly that mistake being
    undone. So the semantic tokens are block *backgrounds* with
    --color-text-primary on them, and every state also carries a gutter glyph, a
    written label and, through the label, something for a screen reader to say.
*@

<article class="@ClassValue" data-testid="@TestId" @attributes="AdditionalAttributes">
    <header class="md-compare__header">
        <div class="md-compare__identity">
            <h3 class="md-compare__name">@FileName</h3>
            <p class="md-compare__meta">@BeforeLabel → @AfterLabel · @SummaryValue</p>
        </div>
        <div class="md-compare__actions">
            @* The one control that makes collapsed-by-default affordable: it
               turns "read the whole file" from N clicks into one. Individual
               folds still work while it is on, because it only supplies the
               default each fold falls back to. Turning it off re-collapses
               everything and discards per-fold state — that is the design, not
               a bug: the alternative is a control whose off state leaves the
               view in a shape neither the reader nor the control chose.

               ToggleButton's text is ChildContent, not a Label parameter; the
               pressed state is on aria-pressed, so no verb flips in the name. *@
            <ToggleButton Pressed="_expandAll"
                          PressedChanged="OnExpandAllChanged"
                          CssClass="md-compare__toggle"
                          TestId="@ToggleTestId">Unchanged sections</ToggleButton>

            @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Path))
            {
                @* Truncated visually when the header is narrow, never in the
                   DOM, so a screen reader still reads the whole path. *@
                <p class="md-compare__path">@Path</p>
            }
        </div>
    </header>

    @* A scroll container is an interactive element even though it is a div:
       without tabindex a keyboard user cannot reach the overflow in Chromium at
       all (.design/accessibility.md, "Non-native controls"). role + name keep it
       from being an unlabelled tab stop.

       The name is "{file}, {before} to {after}" and not just the file. FileView
       can be named by the file alone because it shows one thing and its header
       says which; this body shows a *relationship*. A screen-reader user landing
       here by region navigation, with a compare pane and a plain file view open
       in the same app, would otherwise get two regions both called "README.md"
       and no way to tell which is which — and the difference between them is
       precisely what this component exists for. *@
    <div class="md-compare__body"
         role="region"
         tabindex="0"
         aria-label="@ScrollRegionName"
         style="@BodyStyle"
         data-testid="@BodyTestId">
        @if (Section is null)
        {
            <EmptyState Title="@EmptyTitle"
                        Description="@EmptyDescription"
                        ActionCssClass="@null"
                        CssClass="md-compare__empty"
                        TestId="@EmptyTestId" />
        }
        else
        {
            @* The root is synthetic and has no heading of its own, so its
               preamble and its top-level sections are drawn straight into the
               body rather than inside a section wrapper nobody wrote. *@
            @RenderBlocks(Section.Blocks, "root")
            @RenderChildren(Section, "root", 0)
        }
    </div>
</article>

@code {
    private readonly string _id = "md-compare-" + Guid.NewGuid().ToString("N");

    /// <summary>Per-fold overrides. A key absent from here falls back to
    /// <see cref="_expandAll"/>, which is what lets the header toggle set the
    /// default without having to know every fold on the page.</summary>
    private readonly Dictionary<string, bool> _folds = new(StringComparer.Ordinal);

    private bool _expandAll;

    /// <summary>What the host last asked for, so a re-render for any other
    /// reason does not re-open folds the reader has just closed.</summary>
    private bool? _requestedExpandAll;

    /// <summary>The comparison to draw: the synthetic level-0 root that
    /// <see cref="MarkdownCompare.Compare"/> returns. Null renders the empty
    /// state, for a host whose selection has not landed on a file yet.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public ComparedSection? Section { get; set; }

    /// <summary>The file's name — the title, and the first half of the scroll
    /// region's name.</summary>
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public string FileName { get; set; } = string.Empty;

    /// <summary>The full path, shown opposite the name.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Path { get; set; }

    /// <summary>What the left-hand version is called — "Last commit", "main",
    /// "6e636df". Named by the host, because only the host knows what it read.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string BeforeLabel { get; set; } = "Before";

    /// <summary>What the right-hand version is called — "Working tree".</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string AfterLabel { get; set; } = "After";

    /// <summary>Overrides the counted summary in the header. Null counts what is
    /// in the tree, which is the answer for every host that has not already
    /// worked out a better sentence.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? Summary { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// How many blocks a fold has to hide before it is worth folding. A fold
    /// that hides one paragraph replaces one paragraph with one line of chrome
    /// and one click: it costs the reader more than it saves and makes the view
    /// look busier than the document is.
    /// <para>
    /// The threshold is on the <em>hidden</em> count, not the run length, and
    /// that one rule gives two thresholds: a run of unchanged blocks with a
    /// change on both sides keeps one block of context at each end, so it folds
    /// at four or more; a run at the start or end of a section is bounded on one
    /// side only, keeps one block, and folds at three or more.
    /// </para>
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    public int MinimumHiddenBlocks { get; set; } = 2;

    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the view opens with everything already expanded, which is the
    /// state the header toggle produces.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Off by default, because the request behind this view is "show me what
    /// changed" — see the note on the fold rules further down. It exists for the
    /// host that is not answering that question: a documentation page showing
    /// what each state looks like, or a pane a reader has already told the app
    /// they want to read as a document. Changing it re-seeds the toggle and
    /// discards per-fold state, exactly as pressing the toggle does; reading it
    /// back after the reader has pressed anything would be reading the host's
    /// last instruction, not the view's state, so it is deliberately one-way.
    /// </remarks>
    [Parameter]
    public bool UnchangedExpanded { get; set; }

    /// <summary>How tall the body may grow before it scrolls. Any CSS length;
    /// null lets the body size to its content. Ignored when <see cref="Fill"/>
    /// is set.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public string? MaxHeight { get; set; } = "24rem";

    /// <summary>Take the full height the host gives this and scroll the body
    /// inside it — for a comparison that lives in a pane or a column, where how
    /// tall it gets is the layout's business.</summary>
    [Parameter]
    public bool Fill { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string EmptyTitle { get; set; } = "No file selected";

    [Parameter]
    public string? EmptyDescription { get; set; } = "Choose a file to see what moved in it.";

    [Parameter]
    public string? CssClass { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public string? TestId { get; set; }

    [Parameter(CaptureUnmatchedValues = true)]
    public IReadOnlyDictionary<string, object>? AdditionalAttributes { get; set; }

    protected override void OnParametersSet()
    {
        if (_requestedExpandAll == UnchangedExpanded) return;

        _requestedExpandAll = UnchangedExpanded;
        _expandAll = UnchangedExpanded;
        _folds.Clear();
    }

    /// <summary>Fixed wording, so a test can assert it: "README.md, Last commit
    /// to Working tree".</summary>
    private string ScrollRegionName => $"{FileName}, {BeforeLabel} to {AfterLabel}";

    private string ClassValue
    {
        get
        {
            var fill = Fill ? " md-compare--fill" : string.Empty;
            var extra = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(CssClass) ? string.Empty : " " + CssClass;
            return $"md-compare{fill}{extra}";
        }
    }

    // In fill mode the flex box decides the height, so a max-height here would
    // only fight it.
    private string? BodyStyle =>
        Fill || string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(MaxHeight) ? null : $"max-height: {MaxHeight}";

    private string? BodyTestId => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-body";

    private string? ToggleTestId => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-unchanged";

    private string? EmptyTestId => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-empty";

    private string? FoldTestId(string key) => TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-fold-{key}";

    /// <summary>What the header says after the two version labels: what the host
    /// wrote, or a count of everything the tree reports moved.</summary>
    private string SummaryValue
    {
        get
        {
            if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Summary)) return Summary!;
            if (Section is null) return "Nothing to compare";

            var added = 0;
            var removed = 0;
            var changed = 0;

            Tally(Section, ref added, ref removed, ref changed);

            var parts = new[]
            {
                added > 0 ? $"{added} added" : null,
                removed > 0 ? $"{removed} removed" : null,
                changed > 0 ? $"{changed} changed" : null
            }.Where(part => part is not null);

            // "No changes" rather than "0 added, 0 removed, 0 changed": three
            // zeroes make the reader do arithmetic to reach a two-word answer.
            return parts.Any() ? string.Join(", ", parts) : "No changes";
        }
    }

    /// <summary>Counts headings and blocks together, because a reader asking
    /// "how much moved" is not distinguishing between the two.</summary>
    private static void Tally(ComparedSection section, ref int added, ref int removed, ref int changed)
    {
        foreach (var kind in section.Blocks.Select(block => block.Kind).Append(section.Kind))
        {
            switch (kind)
            {
                case ChangeKind.Added: added++; break;
                case ChangeKind.Removed: removed++; break;
                case ChangeKind.Changed: changed++; break;
            }
        }

        foreach (var child in section.Children) Tally(child, ref added, ref removed, ref changed);
    }

    private bool IsExpanded(string key) => _folds.TryGetValue(key, out var open) ? open : _expandAll;

    private void OnFoldChanged(string key, bool expanded) => _folds[key] = expanded;

    private void OnExpandAllChanged(bool expanded)
    {
        _expandAll = expanded;

        // Per-fold state is discarded either way, so the toggle always means the
        // same thing: everything open, or everything closed. A toggle that
        // remembered exceptions would leave the reader unable to say what
        // pressing it will do.
        _folds.Clear();
    }

    private static int Depth(int depth) => Math.Min(depth, 3);

    private static string Slug(ChangeKind kind) => kind switch
    {
        ChangeKind.Added => "added",
        ChangeKind.Removed => "removed",
        ChangeKind.Changed => "changed",
        _ => "unchanged"
    };

    /// <summary>The gutter glyph. Nothing for Unchanged: silence is the absence
    /// of change, and marking several hundred unchanged blocks would drown the
    /// three that matter.</summary>
    private static string Glyph(ChangeKind kind) => kind switch
    {
        ChangeKind.Added => "+",
        ChangeKind.Removed => "−",
        ChangeKind.Changed => "±",
        _ => string.Empty
    };

    private static string Word(ChangeKind kind) => kind switch
    {
        ChangeKind.Added => "Added",
        ChangeKind.Removed => "Removed",
        ChangeKind.Changed => "Changed",
        _ => string.Empty
    };

    private static string Plural(int count, string noun) => $"{count} {noun}{(count == 1 ? string.Empty : "s")}";
}

@* Sections nest, and there is no breadcrumb. The heading path *is* the nesting;
   printing "Setup › CLI › Windows" above a section whose parents are two rows
   above it repeats what the layout already says. The one case where a breadcrumb
   would earn its place — a deep changed section whose ancestors are folded away —
   cannot occur, because the collapse rule never collapses a section with a
   change beneath it. The rule and the absent breadcrumb are one decision. *@
@code {
    private RenderFragment RenderChildren(ComparedSection section, string key, int depth) =>
        @<text>
            @for (var index = 0; index < section.Children.Count; index++)
            {
                @RenderSection(section.Children[index], $"{key}-{index}", depth + 1)
            }
        </text>;

    private RenderFragment RenderSection(ComparedSection section, string key, int depth) =>
        @<section class="md-compare-section md-compare-section--@Slug(section.Kind)"
                  style="--md-compare-depth: @Depth(depth)"
                  data-testid="@(TestId is null ? null : $"{TestId}-section-{key}")">
            @* Whole-section collapse: the same "hide what did not happen" rule
               one level up. A section with any change anywhere beneath it is
               never collapsed, so a changed grandchild always has its ancestors
               visible and nothing is ever hidden behind a fold that says
               "unchanged". *@
            @if (section.IsWhollyUnchanged && section.Heading is not null)
            {
                <FoldControl BaseClass="md-compare-fold"
                             Expanded="@IsExpanded(key)"
                             ExpandedChanged="@(expanded => OnFoldChanged(key, expanded))"
                             Label="@($"Unchanged — {section.Heading}")"
                             TestId="@FoldTestId(key)">
                    @RenderHeading(section, key)
                    @RenderBlocks(section.Blocks, key)
                    @RenderChildren(section, key, depth)
                </FoldControl>
            }
            else
            {
                @RenderHeading(section, key)
                @RenderBlocks(section.Blocks, key)
                @RenderChildren(section, key, depth)
            }
        </section>;

    /// <summary>
    /// The heading, decorated the same way a block is when it moved.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// Drawn as a <c>p</c> with <c>role="heading"</c> and <c>aria-level</c>, not
    /// as an <c>h2</c> — MarkdownView's existing rule, and its reason applies
    /// unchanged here: "Still a paragraph, so it never joins the host page's own
    /// outline, but carrying its level so heading navigation works at all." A
    /// compare pane inside a storybook page or a desktop workspace must not
    /// inject headings into its host's outline. The <c>md-heading</c> stem is
    /// reused so a heading is sized identically in the compare view and the read
    /// view.
    /// <para>
    /// A Changed heading shows both texts, and only the "Now" line carries the
    /// heading role: two roles would put one section into the outline twice and
    /// leave a screen-reader user navigating by heading landing on a title that
    /// is no longer there.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private RenderFragment RenderHeading(ComparedSection section, string key) =>
        @<div class="md-compare-section__head md-compare-section__head--@Slug(section.Kind)">
            <span class="md-compare-block__gutter" aria-hidden="true">@Glyph(section.Kind)</span>
            <div class="md-compare-block__content">
                @if (section.Kind != ChangeKind.Unchanged)
                {
                    <p class="md-compare-block__label">@Word(section.Kind)</p>
                }

                @if (section.Kind == ChangeKind.Changed)
                {
                    <p class="md-compare-section__was">Was: @section.BeforeHeading</p>
                    <p class="md-heading md-heading--@section.Level md-compare-section__heading md-compare-section__now"
                       id="@($"{_id}-{key}")"
                       role="heading"
                       aria-level="@section.Level">Now: @section.AfterHeading</p>
                }
                else
                {
                    <p class="md-heading md-heading--@section.Level md-compare-section__heading"
                       id="@($"{_id}-{key}")"
                       role="heading"
                       aria-level="@section.Level">@section.Heading</p>
                }
            </div>
        </div>;
}

@* Collapsed by default, and the reason is worth writing down because the
   opposite is the obvious choice.

   The request behind this view is "show me what changed". A comparison that
   opens with ninety per cent unchanged prose is a document viewer with some
   highlights in it, and the reader has to scroll past everything that did not
   happen to find the three things that did. Collapsing by default makes the
   common case — skim what moved — free, and the rare case one keystroke. The
   rejected alternative, expanded-by-default with a "collapse unchanged" control,
   inverts that: it charges the frequent task and subsidises the infrequent one,
   repeatedly, in every long file.

   The cost, named rather than hidden: a reader who wants to read the file as a
   file has to expand, and browser find-in-page will not match text inside a
   collapsed fold, because FoldControl hides its region rather than removing it.
   The header toggle is what keeps that cost to one click.

   FoldControl is reused rather than a second disclosure being written. It
   already gives aria-expanded, aria-controls, a role="region" labelled by its
   own trigger, a chevron that drops its transition under prefers-reduced-motion,
   and a region kept in the DOM. The trigger is a real button and one tab stop in
   reading order between the blocks it separates — these are not a set to choose
   from, so no roving tabindex. Expanding does not move focus: the region appears
   after the trigger, which keeps Shift+Tab meaningful. Nothing auto-collapses on
   blur, and there is no live-region announcement — aria-expanded changing on the
   focused element is already announced, and a polite message on top would say it
   twice.

   The label is the count, with no "Show"/"Hide" verb: aria-expanded already
   carries the state, and a verb that flipped would contradict it and rename the
   trigger mid-interaction. A screen reader hears "12 unchanged blocks,
   collapsed, button." *@
@code {
    private RenderFragment RenderBlocks(IReadOnlyList<ComparedBlock> blocks, string key) => __builder =>
    {
        var index = 0;

        while (index < blocks.Count)
        {
            if (blocks[index].Kind != ChangeKind.Unchanged)
            {
                @RenderBlock(blocks[index])
                index++;
                continue;
            }

            var start = index;
            while (index < blocks.Count && blocks[index].Kind == ChangeKind.Unchanged) index++;
            var end = index;

            // One block of context is kept adjacent to each neighbouring change,
            // so a fold never sits directly against the thing it is context for.
            var keepFirst = start > 0;
            var keepLast = end < blocks.Count;
            var hidden = end - start - (keepFirst ? 1 : 0) - (keepLast ? 1 : 0);

            if (hidden < MinimumHiddenBlocks)
            {
                for (var shown = start; shown < end; shown++)
                {
                    @RenderBlock(blocks[shown])
                }

                continue;
            }

            var foldKey = $"{key}-run{start}";
            var first = keepFirst ? start + 1 : start;
            var last = keepLast ? end - 1 : end;

            if (keepFirst)
            {
                @RenderBlock(blocks[start])
            }

            <FoldControl BaseClass="md-compare-fold"
                         Expanded="@IsExpanded(foldKey)"
                         ExpandedChanged="@(expanded => OnFoldChanged(foldKey, expanded))"
                         Label="@Plural(hidden, "unchanged block")"
                         TestId="@FoldTestId(foldKey)">
                @for (var folded = first; folded < last; folded++)
                {
                    @RenderBlock(blocks[folded])
                }
            </FoldControl>

            if (keepLast)
            {
                @RenderBlock(blocks[end - 1])
            }
        }
    };

    /// <summary>
    /// One block, in up to four redundant channels: the gutter glyph (hidden
    /// from assistive tech), the visible written label, the surface tint, and —
    /// because the label is real DOM text and not an attribute — what the label
    /// makes a screen reader say. A user hears "Added." and then the content,
    /// state before substance, with nothing that can drift out of sync with what
    /// is on screen.
    /// </summary>
    /// <remarks>
    /// There is deliberately no <c>aria-label</c> on the wrapper: it would
    /// either duplicate the visible label or, worse, override the content it
    /// wraps.
    /// <para>
    /// A Changed block shows both halves, stacked, sharing one gutter mark and
    /// one label. Without an intra-block text diff, showing only the "after" and
    /// calling it Changed is an assertion this component cannot back up — the
    /// reader is told something moved and given no way to see what, and for a
    /// one-word edit in a long paragraph the after-only rendering looks
    /// unchanged. Stacking lets the reader's eye do the only comparison
    /// available. Side-by-side columns were rejected independently of scope:
    /// rendered blocks have wildly different heights, so two columns
    /// desynchronise after the first table or code fence, and at pane width they
    /// halve the 72-90 character measure .design/typography-and-layout.md asks
    /// for.
    /// </para>
    /// </remarks>
    private RenderFragment RenderBlock(ComparedBlock block) =>
        @<div class="md-compare-block md-compare-block--@Slug(block.Kind)">
            <span class="md-compare-block__gutter" aria-hidden="true">@Glyph(block.Kind)</span>
            <div class="md-compare-block__content">
                @if (block.Kind != ChangeKind.Unchanged)
                {
                    <p class="md-compare-block__label">@Word(block.Kind)</p>
                }

                @if (block.Kind == ChangeKind.Changed)
                {
                    <div class="md-compare-block__side md-compare-block__side--was">
                        <p class="md-compare-block__side-label">Was</p>
                        <MarkdownView Blocks="@block.Before" />
                    </div>
                    <div class="md-compare-block__side md-compare-block__side--now">
                        <p class="md-compare-block__side-label">Now</p>
                        <MarkdownView Blocks="@block.After" />
                    </div>
                }
                else if (block.Kind == ChangeKind.Removed)
                {
                    <MarkdownView Blocks="@block.Before" />
                }
                else
                {
                    <MarkdownView Blocks="@block.After" />
                }
            </div>
        </div>;
}
